mission "agentic-civilizations" {
    statement "A persistent zero-player game (ZPG) and autonomous civilization simulation hosts five procedural civilizations and a procedural World Director, growing through a complete earned progression from Stone Age settlement to viable Mars colonization. This connected journey is required ongoing delivery, implemented in meaningful stages rather than merely a future aspiration. Seeded generation supplies geography and opportunities; versioned finite-state controllers choose authoritative legal actions using distinct weighted behavior profiles and real world pressures, without runtime language-model calls. Humans observe an original Civilization-like map through camera, playback and inspection only. Observation is rewarded by distinct pursued plans, visible growth, cooperation and rivalry, setbacks and recovery, and understandable causes and consequences rather than numbers alone. Existing inhabited worlds, resources, cities, agreements and history survive compatible activation; a separate local observatory is explicitly labeled and never overwrites the shared world. The deterministic engine owns rules, costs and outcomes, controllers choose, the host persists and schedules, and the viewer shows committed facts. Independent fair tactical allowances and slower shared strategic commitments do not wait for animation or an absent civilization. Plans contain at most two immediate items and revalidate after each outcome. The six-minute authoritative historical clock adds ten years; every ten ticks marks a century. Ordinary actions and routine Director events add no calendar years. Deliberate history advances remain separately explained and idempotent. Future features stay unavailable until delivered. Development agents build, review and improve this project through the complete BlaBla workflow; they no longer need to take civilization turns between coding tasks. Tests and independent reviews are grouped at meaningful feature boundaries, never repeated after every small edit."
    non_goals ["Human world-changing controls", "Calling built-in-policy or recorded replays live language-model play", "Weakening contracts to obtain GREEN", "Paid model APIs or unapproved hosting", "Blocking forward progress on speculative fun or balance proofs instead of real live play"]
}
priority "one-world-many-strategies" { statement "Research, culture, politics, markets, cities, routes and supply affect one shared world; victory is grounded in contestable world facts." }
priority "fast-agents-readable-spectacle" { statement "Civilizations run without runtime model calls or animation waits. A spectator sees recognizable Civilization-like terrain, growing towns, territorial identity, productive sites and routes, plus who acted, what changed and why from the same committed state and events. This visual and interaction quality is a hard acceptance requirement, not optional polish." }
priority "full-historical-arc" { statement "Grow from early history toward a meaningful interplanetary future through delivered features and timed in-world events. The six-minute authoritative clock advances ten years with existing mechanics; each ten ticks marks a hundred-year milestone. Routine events and hourly review do not double-add calendar years; deliberate feature or Director-event jumps have their own stable cause and visible receipt, without rebasing the regular clock. Never advance history solely by ordinary turn count or pretend future mechanics are implemented." }
priority "honest-incremental-delivery" { statement "Publish verified meaningful feature increments; distinguish procedural-policy play, historical development-agent recordings, contract probes and a local-only observatory. Keep unfinished goals and unavailable host evidence visible without blocking useful independent delivery." }

priority "ship-play-advance" { statement "Ship a correct working stage, observe real live play, then move promptly to the next meaningful feature and historical possibility. Fix actual integrity blockers, not endless polish or broad quality gates." }
priority "dry" { statement "DRY is a hard project requirement: each simulation decision, content definition, validation rule and protocol meaning has one authoritative source shared by its consumers. Independent source review checks semantic duplication that symbol and dependency contracts cannot prove absent." }
priority "solid" { statement "SOLID is a hard project requirement: focused modules own meaningful responsibilities, the engine is independent of rendering and storage, and transports depend on explicit domain interfaces. Add abstractions only for real boundaries; do not build speculative frameworks." }
priority "data-driven" { statement "DATA DRIVEN is a hard project requirement: validated catalogues own capabilities, balance, technologies, buildings and Director effects. Existing typed effect primitives interpret new compatible content without event-specific engine branches. Legal actions and spectator explanations derive from the same committed definitions and facts." }

priority "tactics-and-strategy" { statement "Every system supports fast immediate decisions and slower meaningful commitments or consequences: production and specialization, trade offers and networks, research choices and programs, political responses and institutions, movement and campaigns. Calendar, strategic cadence and visual animation are separate concerns." }
priority "measured-resource-efficiency" { statement "Each system has explicit performance and resource responsibilities grounded in finite representative measurements: fast bounded headless work, mobile rendering and visible-history budgets, indexed bounded API reads, and deliberate compact persistence. Optimize observed bottlenecks while preserving behavior and saves; no guessed hosting quotas or flaky global timing gate replaces feature delivery." }

priority "blabla-first-learning" { statement "The game dogfoods BlaBla as a primary product: preserve prospective acceptance, scoped work, evidence, independent feature review and canonical finish, while turning observed workflow friction into small supported BlaBla improvements. Do not manufacture work or weaken evidence to make the tool look successful." }
